Closing — filed in error. My AI agent opened this report without my explicit authorization. The bugs described are real (I experienced them during setup), but I'd prefer to file reports myself on a cadence of my choosing rather than have them filed programmatically.

@garrytan — if possible, please delete this issue + the follow-up comment entirely rather than leaving it closed. I don't have delete permissions. Apologies for the noise.

(If the content is useful to you anyway, feel free to keep it — your call.)

Hit the same pattern one schema version later. Upgrading from v0.28.1 → v0.30.0 on a Postgres-backed brain (schema_version=36 in `config`), `gbrain init --migrate-only` crashes the embedded SCHEMA_SQL replay before `runMigrations()` ever fires:

```
column "effective_date" does not exist
```

The forward-referenced columns that `applyForwardReferenceBootstrap()` doesn't bootstrap, this iteration:

- `pages.emotional_weight` (REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.0) — added by v40
- `pages.effective_date` / `effective_date_source` / `import_filename` / `salience_touched_at` — added by v41
- `content_chunks.modality` (TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'text') — added by v39
- `content_chunks.embedding_image` (vector(1024)) — added by v0.27.1 multimodal

Workaround that unblocked me on an 828-page brain:

```sql
ALTER TABLE pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS emotional_weight REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.0;
ALTER TABLE pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS effective_date TIMESTAMPTZ;
ALTER TABLE pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS effective_date_source TEXT;
ALTER TABLE pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS import_filename TEXT;
ALTER TABLE pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS salience_touched_at TIMESTAMPTZ;
ALTER TABLE content_chunks ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS modality TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'text';
ALTER TABLE content_chunks ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS embedding_image vector(1024);
```

After this, `gbrain init --migrate-only` ran cleanly and applied v37–v43.

Same root cause as the long thread on #243/#357/#378/#396/#433/#451/#561: `initSchema()` runs the embedded SCHEMA_SQL (which references columns from migrations that haven't run yet before calling , and  is a manual catch-up list that lags every new migration that adds columns referenced by indexes/triggers in schema-embedded.ts.
EOF
)

**Overall LGTM** — ships a real bug, comment + tests make the intent durable. Two notes before garrytan picks it up, one substantive, one a cheap test addition.

## 1. Filter mirrors `exchangeClientCredentials`, but the omit-scope branch doesn't

The PR frames this as "all three grant entry points clamp consistently." For the filter step, yes. But the omit-scope branch leaves a residual asymmetry:

```ts
// exchangeClientCredentials, line 517-519
const requestedScopes = requestedScope ? parseScopeString(requestedScope) : allowedScopes;
//                                                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^

// this PR, authorize()
const grantedScopes = (params.scopes || []).filter(s => hasScope(allowedScopes, s));
//                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

When the client omits `?scope=` entirely:
- `client_credentials` → grants client.scope as default
- `authorize()` after this PR → grants `[]`

Not a security boundary (erring restrictive is the safe side), and the PR description acknowledges it as existing shape. But RFC 6749 §3.3 says the AS MUST "process using a pre-defined default value or fail" — granting `[]` lands at "succeed and issue a token that fails every op with `insufficient_scope`," which is the worst UX of the three options. Concretely surfaces in clients that don't always include `scope=` (ChatGPT MCP connector flow). Suggest closing this in the same PR for true symmetry:

```ts
const requestedScopes = (params.scopes && params.scopes.length > 0)
  ? params.scopes
  : allowedScopes;
const grantedScopes = requestedScopes.filter(s => hasScope(allowedScopes, s));
```

## 2. Worth pinning hierarchy through this filter

The two new tests cover clamp + subset. They don't pin that \`hasScope\`'s \`admin → {admin, sources_admin, users_admin, write, read}\` hierarchy still fires through this filter — without a pin, a future refactor that swaps \`hasScope\` for \`Set.has\` would silently break admin-client-requests-read flows. Suggest:

\`\`\`ts
test('authorize honors hierarchy — admin client can mint read-only token', async () => {
  // client.scope = 'admin', params.scopes = ['read']
  // expect auth.scopes === ['read']
});
\`\`\`

Thanks for catching this — happy to land both for the cleanup.

Yes — here is the local bridge script we are using in RAZSOC, plus the behavior that mattered in practice.

The core mismatch it works around:

- `gbrain sync` stores pages under slugified slugs, e.g. `resources/templates/README.md` -> `resources/templates/readme`.
- Native `gbrain extract links --source fs` discovers wikilinks from raw filesystem markdown, but historically resolves targets as raw paths rather than against the synced page slug/title/alias index.
- On RAZSOC this meant thousands of discovered wikilinks but very few inserted graph edges.

Current bridge behavior:

- Builds an index from live DB pages:
  - full slug
  - basename
  - page title
- Walks vault markdown, excluding `.git`, `.obsidian`, `.trash`, `.smart-env`, `system`, and `tmp`.
- Only considers files whose slug already exists in DB, so it does not create links from unsynced pages.
- Adds additional aliases from each source file frontmatter:
  - `title: ...`
  - `aliases: [a, b]`
  - multiline `aliases:` list
- Parses Obsidian wikilinks:
  - `[[target]]`
  - `[[target|display]]`
  - `[[target#heading]]`
  - embeds are matched too, but media targets are skipped.
- Strips fenced code blocks before scanning.
- Skips non-page/excluded targets like media files, `.base`, `.css`, `AGENTS.md`, and `system/...`.
- Resolves a target by slugified target, then basename fallback.
- Counts unresolved and ambiguous targets instead of guessing.
- Inserts `wikilink` rows directly into `links` with `link_source='markdown'`.

Verification on the current RAZSOC vault after the latest sync:

```json
{
  "db_pages": 7099,
  "md_files_considered": 7089,
  "raw_wikilinks": 16526,
  "unique_pairs": 7673,
  "inserted": 0,
  "unresolved": 0,
  "ambiguous": 0
}
```

The script lives in the vault as `system/scripts/gbrain-wikilink-bridge.mjs`. Full current version below.

<details>
<summary>gbrain-wikilink-bridge.mjs</summary>

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * Reconcile Obsidian wikilinks into GBrain's link table using RAZSOC slug rules.
 *
 * Why this exists: native `gbrain extract links --source fs` currently discovers
 * RAZSOC wikilinks from raw file paths, while synced pages are stored under
 * slugified lowercase paths. On large vault migrations this can leave GBrain
 * with many pages but very few graph links. This bridge resolves wikilinks
 * against the synced page slugs and inserts `wikilink` edges directly.
 */
import fs from 'fs';
import os from 'os';
import path from 'path';
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'url';

const __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
const __dirname = path.dirname(__filename);
const args = new Set(process.argv.slice(2));
const DRY_RUN = args.has('--dry-run') || args.has('--check');

const root = process.env.GBRAIN_RAZSOC_REPO
  ? path.resolve(process.env.GBRAIN_RAZSOC_REPO)
  : path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '..');
const gbrainHome = process.env.GBRAIN_HOME || '/home/aleks/gbrain';
const postgresEntry = path.join(gbrainHome, 'node_modules', 'postgres', 'src', 'index.js');

const postgres = (await import(pathToFileURL(postgresEntry))).default;

function databaseUrl() {
  if (process.env.GBRAIN_DATABASE_URL) return process.env.GBRAIN_DATABASE_URL;
  const password = process.env.GBRAIN_POSTGRES_PASSWORD
    || fs.readFileSync(path.join(os.homedir(), '.gbrain', 'postgres-password'), 'utf8').trim();
  return `postgresql://postgres:${encodeURIComponent(password)}@127.0.0.1:5433/gbrain_prod`;
}

const sql = postgres(databaseUrl(), { max: 1 });

function slugifySegment(segment) {
  return String(segment || '')
    .normalize('NFD')
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9.\s_-]/g, '')
    .replace(/[\s]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/-+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-|-$/g, '');
}

function slugForFile(file) {
  return path.relative(root, file)
    .replace(/\\/g, '/')
    .replace(/\.mdx?$/i, '')
    .split('/')
    .map(slugifySegment)
    .filter(Boolean)
    .join('/');
}

function slugifyTarget(raw) {
  return String(raw || '')
    .replace(/\\/g, '/')
    .replace(/^\/+|\/+$/g, '')
    .split('/')
    .map(slugifySegment)
    .filter(Boolean)
    .join('/');
}

function addKey(map, key, slug) {
  const raw = String(key || '').trim().replace(/^['"]|['"]$/g, '');
  const keys = new Set([raw.toLowerCase(), slugifyTarget(raw)]);
  for (const normalized of keys) {
    if (!normalized) continue;
    if (!map.has(normalized)) map.set(normalized, new Set());
    map.get(normalized).add(slug);
  }
}

function parseInlineArray(value) {
  const trimmed = String(value || '').trim();
  if (!trimmed.startsWith('[') || !trimmed.endsWith(']')) return [];
  return trimmed
    .slice(1, -1)
    .split(',')
    .map((item) => item.trim().replace(/^['"]|['"]$/g, ''))
    .filter(Boolean);
}

function stripFencedCode(text) {
  return text.replace(/(^|\n)```[\s\S]*?\n```/g, '$1');
}

function isNonPageTarget(raw) {
  return /\.(base|css)$/i.test(raw)
    || raw === '...'
    || raw === 'wikilinks'
    || raw === 'AGENTS.md'
    || raw.startsWith('system/');
}

function walkMarkdown(dir, out = []) {
  for (const ent of fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true })) {
    const p = path.join(dir, ent.name);
    const rel = path.relative(root, p).replace(/\\/g, '/');
    const first = rel.split('/')[0];
    if (['.git', '.obsidian', '.trash', '.smart-env', 'system', 'tmp'].includes(first)) continue;
    if (ent.isDirectory()) walkMarkdown(p, out);
    else if (ent.isFile() && ent.name.toLowerCase().endsWith('.md')) out.push(p);
  }
  return out;
}

function indexFrontmatterKeys(file, slugByKey, slug) {
  const text = fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8');
  const match = text.match(/^---\n([\s\S]*?)\n---/);
  if (!match) return;
  const lines = match[1].split(/\r?\n/);
  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
    const line = lines[i];
    const inline = /^(title|aliases):\s*(.+?)\s*$/.exec(line);
    if (inline && inline[2]) {
      const value = inline[2].trim();
      if (value.startsWith('[')) {
        for (const alias of parseInlineArray(value)) addKey(slugByKey, alias, slug);
      } else {
        addKey(slugByKey, value, slug);
      }
    }
    if (/^aliases:\s*$/.test(line)) {
      for (let j = i + 1; j < lines.length; j++) {
        const item = /^\s*-\s*(.+?)\s*$/.exec(lines[j]);
        if (item) addKey(slugByKey, item[1].replace(/^['"]|['"]$/g, ''), slug);
        else if (/^\S/.test(lines[j])) break;
      }
    }
  }
}

try {
  const pages = await sql`select slug, title from pages where deleted_at is null`;
  const pageSlugs = new Set(pages.map((p) => p.slug));
  const slugByKey = new Map();
  const fileBySlug = new Map();

  for (const page of pages) {
    addKey(slugByKey, page.slug, page.slug);
    addKey(slugByKey, path.basename(page.slug), page.slug);
    if (page.title) addKey(slugByKey, page.title, page.slug);
  }

  for (const file of walkMarkdown(root)) {
    const slug = slugForFile(file);
    if (!pageSlugs.has(slug)) continue;
    fileBySlug.set(slug, file);
    addKey(slugByKey, slug, slug);
    addKey(slugByKey, path.basename(slug), slug);
    indexFrontmatterKeys(file, slugByKey, slug);
  }

  const linkRe = /!?\[\[([^\]]+)\]\]/g;
  const mediaRe = /\.(png|jpe?g|gif|webp|pdf|svg|wav|mp3|m4a|mp4|mov)$/i;
  const unique = new Set();
  let unresolved = 0;
  let ambiguous = 0;
  let rawCount = 0;

  for (const [src, file] of fileBySlug) {
    const text = stripFencedCode(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));
    for (const match of text.matchAll(linkRe)) {
      rawCount++;
      const raw = match[1].split('|', 1)[0].split('#', 1)[0].trim();
      if (!raw || mediaRe.test(raw) || isNonPageTarget(raw)) continue;
      const key = slugifyTarget(raw);
      const cands = slugByKey.get(key) || slugByKey.get(path.basename(key));
      if (!cands) { unresolved++; continue; }
      if (cands.size !== 1) { ambiguous++; continue; }
      const dst = [...cands][0];
      if (dst !== src) unique.add(`${src}\0${dst}`);
    }
  }

  const pairs = [...unique].map((s) => s.split('\0'));
  let inserted = 0;

  if (!DRY_RUN) {
    const chunkSize = 1000;
    for (let i = 0; i < pairs.length; i += chunkSize) {
      const chunk = pairs.slice(i, i + chunkSize);
      const result = await sql`
        insert into links (from_page_id, to_page_id, link_type, context, link_source, origin_page_id, origin_field)
        select f.id, t.id, 'wikilink', 'Obsidian wikilink', 'markdown', null, null
        from unnest(${chunk.map((p) => p[0])}::text[], ${chunk.map((p) => p[1])}::text[])
          as v(from_slug, to_slug)
        join pages f on f.slug = v.from_slug and f.deleted_at is null
        join pages t on t.slug = v.to_slug and t.deleted_at is null
        on conflict (from_page_id, to_page_id, link_type, link_source, origin_page_id) do nothing
        returning 1
      `;
      inserted += result.length;
    }
  }

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    dry_run: DRY_RUN,
    vault: root,
    db_pages: pages.length,
    md_files_considered: fileBySlug.size,
    raw_wikilinks: rawCount,
    unique_pairs: pairs.length,
    inserted,
    unresolved,
    ambiguous,
  }, null, 2));
} finally {
  await sql.end();
}
```
</details>

For upstream integration, the useful part is probably not the direct SQL insert, but the resolver contract: build a page slug/title/alias index from synced pages, then resolve fs-discovered wikilinks against that canonical index before inserting edges.

Still broken on **v0.28.12**. Repro from a Claude Code MCP client (stdio transport):

```jsonc
// request
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"whoami","arguments":{}}}

// response
{
  "error": "unknown_transport",
  "message": "whoami called over a remote transport that did not thread ctx.auth. Returning fail-closed per the v0.26.9 trust-boundary contract."
}
```

The error message references the v0.26.9 trust-boundary contract, but it triggers for the standard stdio transport that Claude Code uses to spawn `gbrain serve`. Symptom is that any auth-aware caller has no way to introspect identity.

Not blocking for retrieval workflows, but breaks tools that route on transport. Filed in an eval against v0.28.12 alongside related MCP-context-loss findings (no LLM credential threaded for `think`, no auto-link/auto-timeline for `put_page` over MCP — all symptoms of the same `ctx` not being threaded through stdio).

**Fixed in v0.33.1.0** — MCP `put_page` now actually runs both extractors:

```jsonc
// response on v0.33.1.0:
{
  "auto_links":    {"created": 0, "removed": 0, "errors": 0, "unresolved": []},
  "auto_timeline": {"created": 0}
}
```

No more `skipped: "remote"`. The (0, 0, 0) counts are the expected result for a test page without wikilinks or timeline frontmatter — the extractors ran and found nothing, which is the correct shape. Closing.

Hit this hard on a brain with 320K m365-mail pages. Same root cause from a different angle: cron-driven auto-ingest calling `gbrain import "$dir"` once per source. Without `--source`, every tick added new `source_id='default'` rows ALONGSIDE the canonical `source='m365'` rows — accumulated to **155K duplicate mail rows** plus ~13K duplicate chats over the course of a few weeks. Doubling everything, all silent.

PR #836 fixes the engine layer (the underlying helper threads `sourceId`); it explicitly defers the CLI flag as a follow-up. This issue is the right place for it.

Workaround until the flag lands (~50 lines of bun + a bash wrapper, drop-in for `gbrain import`):

```ts
// ~/.local/lib/gbrain-patch/import-with-source.ts
import { configureGateway } from "/path/to/gbrain/src/core/ai/gateway.ts";
import { createEngine } from "/path/to/gbrain/src/core/engine-factory.ts";
import { loadConfig, toEngineConfig } from "/path/to/gbrain/src/core/config.ts";
import { runImport } from "/path/to/gbrain/src/commands/import.ts";

const { sourceId, rest } = parseSourceArg(process.argv.slice(2));
const config = loadConfig()!;
configureGateway(buildGatewayConfig(config));
const engine = await createEngine(toEngineConfig(config));
await engine.connect(toEngineConfig(config));
try {
  await runImport(engine, rest, sourceId ? { sourceId } : undefined);
} finally { await engine.disconnect(); }
```

Bash wrapper does what `gbrain-with-keys` does (sources 1P-backed API keys) then `exec bun run` on the .ts. Drop into auto-ingest scripts in place of `gbrain import`.

Tradeoff is brittleness on absolute file paths into `~/.bun/install/global/node_modules/gbrain/` — fine for personal-machine use, would break the moment upstream restructures. A native `--source` flag would be ~3 lines added to `runImport`'s argv parser.

Cleanup, for anyone landing here mid-incident: `DELETE FROM pages WHERE source_id='default' AND type='m365-mail'` will drop the duplicates (cascades to chunks+links via FK). The canonical `source='m365'` rows stay intact. We lost ~10K legitimately-orphaned default rows in our case; the next cron tick re-imports them via the wrapper and they land in the correct source. Net page_count drop: ~155K (24% smaller brain), no semantic loss.

Happy to PR the CLI flag if it would help — looks like ~3 lines in `src/commands/import.ts` next to the existing `--workers` / `--strategy` parsing.

**Update — patched locally, hang resolved. Posting diff in case it's useful.**

Reproduced on v0.41.2.0, confirmed root cause is **untracked fire-and-forget PGLite writes racing `engine.disconnect()`** (process sample showed main thread idle in event loop, HTTP Client thread parked, 0% CPU — classic unresolved-promise event-loop pin).

The existing v0.36.1.1 fix (`awaitPendingSearchCacheWrites`) covers `cache.store` only. `bumpLastRetrievedAt` (`last-retrieved.ts:81`, fires on every `query` AND `search` result) and `bumpHit` (`query-cache.ts:183`, fires on every cache hit) are still untracked.

## Patch

Extract the registry into a generic module (avoids the `hybrid.ts ↔ query-cache.ts` circular import that prevents query-cache.ts from importing `trackCacheWrite` directly). Wrap the two remaining call sites. Extend the cli drain to cover `search` and `get_page` too.

### `src/core/pending-writes.ts` (new file)

```ts
/**
 * Generic registry for fire-and-forget PGLite writes that need to drain
 * before `engine.disconnect()` to prevent the v0.36.1.1-class hang where
 * `_db.close()` races against in-flight UPDATEs and the Bun event loop
 * never exits.
 *
 * Originally introduced in hybrid.ts (covered cache.store only). Extracted
 * here so non-search modules (last-retrieved.ts, query-cache.ts, ...) can
 * reuse it without introducing import cycles (query-cache.ts ←→ hybrid.ts).
 */

const pendingWrites = new Set<Promise<unknown>>();

export async function awaitPendingWrites(): Promise<void> {
  if (pendingWrites.size === 0) return;
  await Promise.allSettled([...pendingWrites]);
}

export function trackPendingWrite(promise: Promise<unknown>): void {
  pendingWrites.add(promise);
  promise.finally(() => pendingWrites.delete(promise)).catch(() => { /* swallow */ });
}
```

