Related finding: I identified a specific case where the lint phase within an autopilot cycle triggers the same module-level singleton disconnect pattern.

In `src/commands/lint.ts`, `resolveLintContentSanity()` calls `engine.connect({})` which connects to the module-level singleton pool. When `engine.disconnect()` runs in the `finally` block, it tears down that shared pool, breaking any subsequent phase that depends on the DB connection.

**Fix:** Using `engine.connect({ poolSize: 2 })` creates an instance-level pool that `disconnect()` can safely close without affecting the module singleton:

```typescript
// Before (line 315)
await engine.connect({});

// After  
await engine.connect({ poolSize: 2 });
```

This is a targeted fix for the lint phase specifically. The broader dream-cycle singleton disconnect pattern may need additional guards in other phases.

Hit this exact `No database connection: connect() has not been called` class in production on a long-running `gbrain autopilot` daemon (v0.42.1.0, instance-pool engine against a shared local Postgres). Wanted to flag a piece that sits *underneath* this PR, because on current `local/integration` the `batchRetry` reconnect callback (`reconnect: () => this.reconnect()`) is already wired in — yet the crash still happens. The remaining defect is in `reconnect()` itself.

**Symptom:** worker runs a full cycle's early phases fine, then late phases throw `No database connection`, `executeJob` crashes unhandled, worker exits code=1, respawns, repeats every ~5 min. `conversation_facts_backfill` is the phase that crashes the worker, but it's a red herring — it's default-OFF, so its only DB touch is `loadCfg`'s `engine.getConfig() x7`, i.e. it's just the *first hard call after the engine already died mid-cycle*. The `Promotion error: No database connection` lines from the worker's `promoteDelayed()` loop confirm `_sql` was already null process-wide.

**Root cause:** `PostgresEngine.reconnect()` disconnects the live pool *before* attempting `connect()`:

```js
try { await this.disconnect(); } catch {}   // _sql = null HERE
await this.connect(this._savedConfig);        // if THIS throws, _sql stays null forever
```

So when a transient blip fires the retry→reconnect path and Postgres is still unreachable for those few seconds, `connect()` throws and `_sql` is left null permanently. Every subsequent call uses the accessor `this._sql || db.getConnection()`; with `_sql` null it falls to the module singleton, which the autopilot process never connected → throw. Non-bulk methods (`getConfig`, per-phase reads) have no retry wrapper, so the first one after the failed reconnect throws unhandled and kills the worker.

This is why wiring reconnect into batchRetry (this PR) is necessary but not sufficient: the reconnect it calls can itself brick the engine.

**Fix that worked for us — build-then-swap** (don't tear down the old pool until the new one is proven live; restore on failure so the next query self-heals):

```js
async reconnect(): Promise<void> {
  if (!this._savedConfig || this._reconnecting) return;
  this._reconnecting = true;
  const oldSql = this._sql;
  const oldManager = this.connectionManager;
  try {
    this._sql = null;                       // force connect() to build fresh
    await this.connect(this._savedConfig);  // validates via SELECT 1 before returning
    if (oldSql) { try { await oldSql.end({ timeout: 5 }); } catch {} }
  } catch (err) {
    if (this._sql && this._sql !== oldSql) { try { await this._sql.end({ timeout: 5 }); } catch {} }
    this._sql = oldSql;
    this.connectionManager = oldManager;
    throw err;                              // let batchRetry's backoff handle it
  } finally {
    this._reconnecting = false;
  }
}
```

Green on `test/core/retry-reconnect.test.ts` + `retry.test.ts` (42 pass), `test/e2e/db-singleton-shared-recovery.test.ts` (3 pass), and the disconnect-idempotency e2e (7 pass). Looks complementary to the module-singleton refcount work in #1754/#1758 too — that layer and this instance-pool layer are orthogonal; both seem needed. Happy to fold this into whichever PR lands as the canonical fix.

Hit the same bug today on **0.42.1.0** (macOS, Bun, self-hosted Postgres 16 reached over Tailscale at ~120ms RTT, Voyage embeddings). Traced it to root cause and have a verified one-move fix.

## Root cause

The 10s "disconnect hang guard" in `src/cli.ts` is installed at the **top of main, before the op handler runs**:

```ts
const DISCONNECT_HARD_DEADLINE_MS = 10_000;
let forceExitTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
if (shouldForceExitAfterMain()) {
  forceExitTimer = setTimeout(() => {
    console.warn(`[cli] engine.disconnect() did not return within ${DISCONNECT_HARD_DEADLINE_MS}ms — force-exiting`);
    process.exit(0);
  }, DISCONNECT_HARD_DEADLINE_MS);
  forceExitTimer.unref?.();
}
```

`clearTimeout` only happens in the `finally` block AFTER `engine.disconnect()` returns. So it is accidentally a **whole-run timer, not a disconnect guard**: any command whose total wall time exceeds 10s gets `process.exit(0)` mid-handler. Result is exactly what you see: empty stdout, exit 0, and the misleading "disconnect did not return" warning even though disconnect was never the problem.

This is why only `search`/`query` are affected: they are the only hot paths that stack query embedding + LLM multi-query expansion + several DB round trips + the 8s reranker timeout fallback. On a remote Postgres brain that pipeline routinely crosses 10s. `list`/`get`/`doctor` finish in under 10s so the timer never fires. Same reason 0.22.8 works: that era had no force-exit timer. And it explains your "data is healthy" evidence: the search SQL executes and commits fine server-side (I confirmed via `log_statement='all'`), and the rerank-failures audit log shows docs were retrieved. The process is killed between retrieval and `formatResult`.

Two corroborating observations:

- `gbrain call query '{"query":"...","limit":2}'` **works** on the same brain, because the raw call path skips expansion and finishes under 10s.
- The timing budget interacts with #1693 (config round-trips inflating query latency on remote Postgres). Fixing #1693 would mask this for some setups, but any sufficiently slow link still trips it.

## Fix (verified locally)

Move the timer installation into the `finally` block, immediately before `await engine.disconnect()`, keeping the declaration at top:

```ts
} finally {
  try { /* facts queue drain, unchanged */ } catch {}
  if (shouldForceExitAfterMain()) {
    forceExitTimer = setTimeout(() => { /* same warn + exit */ }, DISCONNECT_HARD_DEADLINE_MS);
    forceExitTimer.unref?.();
  }
  await engine.disconnect();
  if (forceExitTimer) clearTimeout(forceExitTimer);
```

The 10s deadline then measures disconnect only, which is what the defense-in-depth comment (adversarial-review C13) describes it as guarding. After this patch, `gbrain query` on my remote Postgres brain prints ranked results again (~15-20s total run) and fast commands are unchanged. A hung handler loses its 10s whole-run backstop, but the op layer already carries its own timeouts (`SET LOCAL statement_timeout`, reranker timeout, withTimeout wrappers), so disconnect-only scope seems like the right tradeoff.

Happy to turn this into a PR if useful.

I can reproduce this on a different version (0.32.0) with 262 pages on PGLite, same error: `GBrain: Timed out waiting for PGLite lock.` 0 other comments and no PR — opening a workaround + root-cause analysis here.

## The error message is misleading

`reindex-frontmatter` never actually waits on `acquireLock(dataDir, { timeoutMs: 30_000 })`. I confirmed this by:

1. Watching `~/.gbrain/brain.pglite/.gbrain-lock/` while running the command — lock dir gets created in ~6s, command exits at ~33s, lock dir is gone (released cleanly).
2. Running the same lock code in isolation — `acquireLock` returns in 1ms with the lock dir absent.
3. Calling `src/core/backfill-effective-date.ts` directly from a Bun script, bypassing the CLI dispatch — same wait, same exit, plus an actual SQL error that the CLI's outer `try/catch` swallows before `process.exit(0)`.

The real cause is **PGLite's default `statement_timeout = 30s` firing inside the `engine.transaction()` block** in `backfill-effective-date.ts`:

```
error: attempted to delete invisible tuple
   length: 92, severity: "ERROR", code: "55000"
   routine: "heap_delete", file: "heapam.c"
   query: "UPDATE pages SET effective_date = $1::timestamptz,
                             effective_date_source = $2 WHERE id = $3"
```

`SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '600s'` (src/core/backfill-effective-date.ts:183) is gated on `engine.kind === 'postgres'`, so PGLite never gets the boost. The comment says PGLite "doesn't have the timeout problem in the first place (single writer)" — but it does inherit PGLite's session default (30s). On any non-trivial batch (mine: 262 pages, report's: 76 pages), the UPDATE statement inside the SET-LOCAL-less PGLite transaction trips statement_timeout. PGLite then surfaces this as `attempted to delete invisible tuple` (heapam.c) because the transaction is in a confused state when the timeout fires mid-UPDATE.

The CLI's exit code is `0`, not `1` — `reindex-frontmatter` is dispatched in cli.ts:1166 inside a generic catch wrapper that swallows the error. The shell sees "Timed out waiting for PGLite lock" on stderr (which is actually the PGLite heap_delete error repackaged via the same lock-watcher error path) and exit 0, with `effective_date_source` unchanged.

## Workaround that works for me

Bypass the broken `gbrain reindex-frontmatter` CLI and run per-row UPDATEs without a transaction wrapper. The bug is in the transaction-based batch update; single-row UPDATEs are fine.

```ts
// list_effdate.mjs
import { createEngine } from 'gbrain/src/core/engine-factory.ts';
import { loadConfig, toEngineConfig } from 'gbrain/src/core/config.ts';

const cfg = loadConfig();
const engine = await createEngine(toEngineConfig(cfg));
await engine.connect(toEngineConfig(cfg));

const result = await engine.executeRaw(
  `SELECT id FROM pages
    WHERE deleted_at IS NULL
      AND effective_date_source = 'fallback'
      AND (frontmatter ? 'event_date' OR frontmatter ? 'date' OR frontmatter ? 'published')`,
);
for (const r of result.rows) {
  // recompute and UPDATE one row at a time, no transaction
  await engine.executeRaw(
    `UPDATE pages SET effective_date = $1, effective_date_source = $2 WHERE id = $3`,
    [computedDate.toISOString(), computedSource, r.id],
  );
}
await engine.disconnect();
```

## One more finding — `effective_date_health` is itself noisy

`src/commands/doctor.ts:1599-1615` reports `fallback_with_fm_date` based on `frontmatter ? 'date'` (key exists in JSONB), NOT on whether `parseDateLoose` can actually parse the value. In my run, 3 pages were flagged but all 3 had malformed date values like `"2026-02-12T1023"` (HHMM no colons — flomo ingest pipeline issue). Even if `reindex-frontmatter` worked, those 3 pages would still be fallback. The check should also exclude rows where `parseDateLoose(frontmatter->>'date')` returns null to avoid false positives.

## Suggested fix in `backfill-effective-date.ts`

Either:
- Add `if (isPostgres) ... else { await tx.executeRaw(\`SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '1800s'\`); }` so PGLite gets the boost too.
- Or skip the transaction wrapper on PGLite and run per-row UPDATEs, since PGLite is single-writer anyway.

Both make the CLI work. The misleading error message in cli.ts:1166-1178 (no rethrow, no proper logging) should also be fixed.

**Re: the "requires `ignore` npm package" exclusion in #1253**

Just want to flag for the record that this PR as it stands adds **no npm dependencies**. The diff touches only `src/cli.ts`, `src/commands/import.ts`, `src/commands/sync.ts`, and `test/import-walker.test.ts` — there's no `package.json` change. The gitignore set is built by shelling out to git:

```ts
execFileSync('git', ['-C', root, 'ls-files', '-o', '-i', '--exclude-standard', '--directory'], …)
```

`execFileSync` is a Node/Bun builtin (`child_process`), and `git` is already an assumed runtime tool everywhere else in the sync path (the incremental walker uses `git ls-files --exclude-standard` today). So the "no new deps" gate that excluded this in the v0.37.7.0 wave should actually pass — there's nothing to vendor. I think the exclusion may have conflated this PR with the broader `.gbrainignore` / #449 work, which *would* want a glob library for non-git roots.

**Two paths, your call:**

**Path A — keep the `git` subprocess (this PR).** Zero npm deps, correct gitignore semantics for free (negations, `**`, `.git/info/exclude`, global excludes), and directory-level pruning via `--directory`. The one constraint is it only fires inside a git work tree — non-git roots fall back to legacy behavior, which this PR already documents as intentional.

**Path B — if you'd rather not depend on the `git` executable at all,** the same intent can be done in-repo without a glob library, by reading `.gitignore` and matching it ourselves. Rough sketch:

```ts
// Parse .gitignore (+ .git/info/exclude) into ordered rules.
type Rule = { re: RegExp; negate: boolean; dirOnly: boolean };

function loadRules(root: string): Rule[] {
  const rules: Rule[] = [];
  for (const line of readIgnoreLines(root)) {           // skip '' and '#...'
    let pat = line.trim();
    if (!pat || pat.startsWith('#')) continue;
    const negate = pat.startsWith('!'); if (negate) pat = pat.slice(1);
    const dirOnly = pat.endsWith('/'); if (dirOnly) pat = pat.replace(/\/$/, '');
    const anchored = pat.startsWith('/'); if (anchored) pat = pat.slice(1);
    rules.push({ re: globToRegExp(pat, anchored), negate, dirOnly });
  }
  return rules;
}

// gitignore glob -> RegExp:  ** = any depth, * = within a segment, ? = one char.
// Unanchored patterns match at any path depth; anchored match from root.
function globToRegExp(pat: string, anchored: boolean): RegExp {
  const body = pat
    .replace(/[.+^${}()|[\]\\]/g, '\\$&')   // escape regex metachars
    .replace(/\*\*\//g, '(?:.*/)?')          // **/  -> any number of dirs
    .replace(/\*\*/g, '.*')                  // **   -> anything
    .replace(/\*/g, '[^/]*')                 // *    -> within one segment
    .replace(/\?/g, '[^/]');
  const prefix = anchored ? '^' : '(?:^|/)';
  return new RegExp(`${prefix}${body}(?:/.*)?$`);        // dir matches its children
}

// Last matching rule wins (gitignore precedence); negation re-includes.
function isIgnored(relPath: string, isDir: boolean, rules: Rule[]): boolean {
  let ignored = false;
  for (const r of rules) {
    if (r.dirOnly && !isDir) continue;
    if (r.re.test(relPath)) ignored = !r.negate;
  }
  return ignored;
}
```

Then in the walker, prune at descent time exactly like the current PR:

```ts
const rel = relative(root, full);
if (isIgnored(rel, isDirEntry, rules)) continue;
```

That's ~40 lines, no dependency, and gives the same directory-pruning win for both git and non-git roots — at the cost of owning a (small) slice of gitignore semantics ourselves. It won't be 100% spec-complete the way `git` is (e.g. nested per-directory `.gitignore` files, some edge cases), but it covers the `dist/`/`coverage/`/`out/` pain from #1073.

Happy to push either shape — Path A is already in the diff and is the lower-risk merge; Path B if you want to drop the `git` executable assumption entirely. Either way it lands without a new npm dep.

