Adding an explicitly minimal fix option, since "reopen #1681" (gateway routing) is a bigger ask:

## Option: strip + loud guard (~10 lines, both call sites)

Plain prefix-stripping (#1401's approach) has a hole: the call sites hardcode `new Anthropic()`, so stripping `openai:gpt-4o` → `gpt-4o` still sends an OpenAI model to the Anthropic API — the prefix told you which client to use, and stripping throws that information away. Silent mis-route, and the extractor's fail-open behavior hides it.

The minimal *correct* version adds a provider check using the helper that already exists (`splitProviderModelId`, `src/core/model-id.ts`):

```ts
import { splitProviderModelId } from '../core/model-id.ts';

function toAnthropicModelId(resolved: string, context: string): string {
  const { provider, model } = splitProviderModelId(resolved);
  if (provider && provider.trim().toLowerCase() !== 'anthropic') {
    throw new Error(
      `${context}: resolved model "${resolved}" targets provider "${provider}", ` +
      `but this code path calls the Anthropic SDK directly. ` +
      `Configure an anthropic:* model (or a bare Anthropic id), or route via the gateway.`,
    );
  }
  return model; // bare id — what the SDK expects
}
```

Apply at both resolution sites in `src/commands/eval-longmemeval.ts` (answer-gen `model`, trajectory `extractorModel`). Behavior matrix:

| resolved value | today | with strip+guard |
|---|---|---|
| `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` (the default!) | 404 | ✅ works |
| bare `claude-sonnet-4-6` (flag/env) | works | works |
| `openai:gpt-4o` | 404 (answer-gen) / silent no-op (extractor) | **loud error** naming the misconfiguration |

This fixes the out-of-the-box 404, eliminates the extractor's silent fail-open for the misconfigured-provider case, and doesn't preclude the gateway routing in #1681 later — it just stops the bleeding at minimal review cost. The same shape would close the sibling issues (#1857, #1307) at their call sites.

Confirmed on **v0.41.14.0**. A bare `gbrain dream` on Postgres exits 0 but **13 of 15 phases fail** with `No database connection: connect() has not been called`; only the filesystem-only phases (`lint`, `backlinks`, `extract`) pass. The clobber lands at the tail of the **first** phase (`lint`), exactly as you describe.

Mechanism confirmed: `connectEngine()` → `toEngineConfig()` never sets `poolSize`, so the cycle's main engine takes `PostgresEngine.connect`'s **module branch** and *is* the `db.ts` global singleton. `resolveLintContentSanity` then opens a second engine, also module-mode (`engine.connect({})`), and its `finally { disconnect() }` — `_sql === null`, `_connectionStyle === 'module'` — falls through to `db.disconnect()` → `sql.end(); sql = null`. Every later phase's `this.sql` → `db.getConnection()` then throws.

**Minimal fix** (tested — dream goes from 2/15 → 15/15 phases): give the throwaway lint engine its own pool, so its `disconnect()` ends only `_sql` and can never reach `db.disconnect()`.

```diff
       try {
-        await engine.connect({});
+        // Instance mode (own _sql pool): this throwaway engine's disconnect()
+        // ends only _sql and never falls through to db.disconnect(), which
+        // would clobber the module singleton the dream cycle's main engine
+        // is sharing.
+        await engine.connect({
+          engine: base!.engine,
+          database_url: base!.database_url,
+          database_path: base!.database_path,
+          poolSize: 1,
+        });
         const lifted = await loadConfigWithEngine(engine, base);
         cs = lifted?.content_sanity ?? cs;
       } finally {
         await engine.disconnect().catch(() => { /* best-effort cleanup */ });
       }
```

Passing the real `database_url`/`database_path` is required — the instance branch throws on a missing URL, whereas `connect({})` only worked before because the global was already up.

Note this also unblocks #1973: the per-source `last_full_cycle_at` write is gated on `status === 'ok' | 'clean' | 'partial'`, so until the phases stop failing, the freshness stamp is skipped regardless.

Confirmed on **v0.41.14.0**, with a CLI-path root cause worth adding: **`src/commands/dream.ts` never parses `--source`**. `runDream`'s arg parser has no `--source` case, and the `runCycle({...})` opts object it builds has no `sourceId` field. So `gbrain dream --source X` silently drops the flag, `runCycle` runs with `opts.sourceId` unset, and the `last_full_cycle_at` write in `cycle.ts` (gated on `opts.sourceId`, only ever set by autopilot's per-source fanout) is skipped. The cycle does all the work but never stamps the watermark — which also makes doctor's own `cycle_freshness` fix hint ("Run `gbrain dream --source <id>`") impossible to follow.

**Fix** (tested — `gbrain dream --source default` then stamps `last_full_cycle_at` and `cycle_freshness` clears):

In the arg parser:
```diff
+  const sourceIdx = args.indexOf('--source');
+  const source = sourceIdx !== -1 ? args[sourceIdx + 1] ?? null : null;
   return {
     json: args.includes('--json'),
     ...
+    source,
     bypassDreamGuard: args.includes('--unsafe-bypass-dream-guard'),
   };
```

In `runDream`'s `runCycle` call:
```diff
   const report = await runCycle(engine, {
     brainDir,
     dryRun: opts.dryRun,
     pull: opts.pull,
     phases,
+    sourceId: opts.source ?? undefined,
     synthInputFile: opts.inputFile ?? undefined,
     ...
   });
```

Caveat: on a Postgres brain this only takes effect once #1471 is fixed — otherwise the cycle's DB phases fail, `status` becomes `failed`, and the stamp (gated on `ok | clean | partial`) is skipped anyway. The two are coupled.

**Found the root cause — it's not a crash, it's a *swallowed* dirty-tree refusal.** (Follow-up to my earlier comment.)

Reading `src/commands/migrations/v0_32_2.ts`, **Phase B (`phaseBFenceFacts`)** does a "dirty-tree refusal" before writing fences:

```ts
for (const [id, localPath] of localPathById) {
  if (localPath && isLocalPathDirty(localPath)) {
    return {
      name: 'fence_facts',
      status: 'failed',
      detail: `source "${id}" has uncommitted changes in ${localPath}. Commit or stash, then re-run.`,
    };
  }
}
```

My brain's `default` source `local_path` had **713 uncommitted files** (the engine's markdown write-through + EOL churn keep the mirror dirty), so the migration correctly refused — **but `gbrain apply-migrations` only prints `status=failed` and never surfaces the phase `detail`, and `~/.gbrain/upgrade-errors.jsonl` is never written.** So the actionable message is invisible and it reads like a silent crash.

This also explains the "fails even with `facts` at 0 rows" point from the original report: the dirty-tree check runs **unconditionally, before** any fact-count logic, so a 0-fact brain still fails if the source tree is dirty.

**Repro → fix on my end:** `git -C <source local_path> stash -u` → `gbrain apply-migrations --yes` → `Migration v0.32.2 complete` instantly. (Then `gbrain dream --source <id>` to set `last_full_cycle_at` and clear the downstream `cycle_freshness` failure.)

**Suggested fixes (cheap):**
1. **Surface the phase `detail` on orchestrator failure** — it's already a perfect actionable message ("source X has uncommitted changes… Commit or stash"); the runner just needs to print it and/or write `upgrade-errors.jsonl`. That alone makes this self-diagnosable.
2. Optionally skip the dirty-tree refusal when there's nothing to fence (`facts WHERE row_num IS NULL` = 0) — Phase B won't touch the filesystem in that case anyway.

Happy to send a PR for (1) if that helps.

Confirmed on `v0.41.4.0` with bare-date daily notes (`# 2026-05-25`, no frontmatter). Tracked down the exact source and have a tested fix.

### Root cause

It's the **inference serializer**, not just the sanity check. `serializeFrontmatter` in `src/core/frontmatter-inference.ts` only quotes titles containing explicit special chars:

```ts
const needsQuote = /[:"'#\[\]{}|>&*!?,]/.test(fm.title);
lines.push(`title: ${needsQuote ? JSON.stringify(fm.title) : fm.title}`);
```

A bare date has none of those chars, so it's emitted unquoted as `title: 2026-05-25`. `applyInference` prepends that block and `parseMarkdown` (gray-matter / js-yaml) coerces the bare date into a `Date`. Then `content-sanity.ts:238` `opts.title.toLowerCase()` throws.

Minimal repro:

```ts
import { applyInference } from "./src/core/frontmatter-inference.ts";
import matter from "gray-matter";
const { content } = applyInference("Calendar/Days/2026-05-25.md", "# 2026-05-25\n");
console.log(matter(content).data.title instanceof Date); // true
```

### Fix

Quote any title YAML would read back as a non-string scalar (dates, numbers, times, reserved literals):

```ts
const looksLikeNonString =
  /^[\d.+-]/.test(fm.title) ||
  /^(true|false|yes|no|on|off|null|~)$/i.test(fm.title.trim());
const needsQuote = /[:"'#\[\]{}|>&*!?,]/.test(fm.title) || looksLikeNonString;
```

After this, the title serializes as `title: "2026-05-25"` and round-trips as a string. Re-ran `import` + `embed --stale` on the affected vault: 0 errors, both date-named notes indexed.

A `String(opts.title)` guard at the `content-sanity` boundary is still worth adding as defense-in-depth (covers any other path that hands it a non-string), but fixing the serializer addresses the root cause for inferred frontmatter.

Additional confirmation from a production Postgres/macOS setup on `gbrain 0.42.52.0`.

This still reproduces in the broader form: a real cycle can complete and do useful work, while `doctor` remains stale if the cycle is not source-scoped.

Observed sequence:

1. `gbrain dream --json` completed a real write cycle:
