- `dead`/`cancelled` idempotency hits should not permanently suppress re-submission for the same transcript/chunk.
- A child outcome of `cancelled` should not count as successfully synthesized work.
- If all children are `cancelled`/`dead` and `pages_written = 0`, the phase should be `failed` or `partial/degraded`, not `ok`, and should not advance the synth completion timestamp as if the work succeeded.

#2253 looks like it addresses the root idempotency slot problem. This repro is mainly to confirm the user-visible impact: GBrain can currently say “synthesized” with zero pages written because cancelled children were treated as terminal/handled.

Hit this exact bug today on a two-source PGLite brain (`default` + an Obsidian vault registered as its own source via `sources add` + `import --source-id`). Root-caused it to a specific silent-drop mechanism, patched locally, and verified — details + diff below in case useful. Line refs are against v0.42.53.0 (`814258dd`); I checked current `master` and the affected push sites are still unstamped (`extract.ts:1036`, `extract.ts:1134`), so this should still apply.

## Symptom

- `link_resolution.global_basename` = `true`
- `gbrain extract all --source-id <vault> --json` → `{"links_created": 0, "timeline_entries_created": 0, "pages_processed": 1024}`
- Same zero via the `dream` cycle's extract phase (both incremental and full-walk).
- Meanwhile `doctor`'s `link_resolution_opportunity` said ~1878/1882 bare wikilinks *would* resolve — and `--dry-run` agreed: **"would create 984"**. Real run: **0, with zero errors emitted**.

## Root cause

The FS-walk extract paths build batch rows **without source ids**, and the engine's batch write JOINs pages on `(slug, source_id)` — so for any non-default source, every row misses the JOIN and inserts nothing. No exception, no batch error; the failure is invisible.

Chain (v0.42.53.0):

1. `src/commands/extract.ts` — all three FS-walk extractors (`extractLinksFromDir`, `extractForSlugs`, `extractTimelineFromDir`) push `LinkBatchInput` / `TimelineBatchInput` rows with no `from_source_id` / `to_source_id` / `source_id`.
2. `src/core/batch-rows.ts:140-142` — omitted source ids default to `'default'`.
3. `src/core/pglite-engine.ts:2529-2530` — `addLinksBatch` inserts via `JOIN pages ... AND f.source_id = v.from_source_id` (and the `to` side likewise). Vault pages carry the vault's source id; the rows said `default`; inner JOIN matches nothing → 0 rows written, silently.
4. `src/core/engine.ts:146-157` even documents this hazard on the batch-input types — the FS-walk callers just predate multi-source and were never updated (the sync-time extract hooks at `extract.ts:~1227` DO stamp source ids correctly; the standalone walk paths don't).

Compounding bug: `--source-id` is parsed (`extract.ts:668-669`) but on the FS path it was **only** wired into the DB-source branches (`extract.ts:901/906`) — on the default `--source fs` path it's silently ignored, so extraction walks whatever dir the default-source resolver picks and stamps nothing. It only walked my vault at all because the "sole non-default source" fallback happened to pick it.

This also explains why the issue title says "link resolution appears to target `default`" — resolution is actually fine (dry-run proves it); it's the **write** that targets `default`.

## Fix (local patch, verified)

Three parts, all in `src/commands/extract.ts` (+98/−9):

1. New `resolveSourceIdForDir(engine, dir)` — maps the walked dir to the registered source owning it (longest `local_path` containment match, normalized separators, case-insensitive on win32; falls back to `'default'`, never throws).
2. `runExtractCore` resolves the source once (`opts.sourceId ?? await resolveSourceIdForDir(...)`) and passes it through all three FS-walk extractors, which stamp every link row (`from_source_id`/`to_source_id`) and timeline row (`source_id`). Because the cycle path (`core/cycle.ts` → `runExtractCore`) funnels through the same choke point, this fixes CLI, dream cycle, and jobs in one place.
3. `--source-id` honored on the FS path: selects that source's `local_path` to walk (clear errors when the source doesn't exist / has no local_path) and stamps rows with it.

Verification on the affected brain:

- Same failing command now: `links_created: 983` (984 dry-run minus one dangling endpoint), `graph-query` shows real `wikilink_basename` edges (daily note → `sessions/<page>`), immediate re-run creates 0 (dedupe intact).
- Cycle path re-tested live: deleted one edge via the engine API, re-ran the incremental extract exactly as the cycle calls it (no explicit sourceId) — the dir→source resolution kicked in and recreated the edge.
- `tsc --noEmit` clean; extract test suite identical pass/fail with and without the patch (the 10 failures are pre-existing win32 path-separator assumptions, unrelated).

