Confirmed on a Supabase Postgres brain upgrading 0.42.45.0 → 0.42.57.0 (schema v117 → v122): `gbrain init --migrate-only` fails with `column "event_page_id" does not exist` before any migration runs — the embedded SCHEMA_SQL replay hits `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_timeline_event_page ON timeline_entries(event_page_id)` while the table still has its pre-v121 shape, and `applyForwardReferenceBootstrap` doesn't cover this column (its coverage ends at v0.27-era columns).

Clean workaround that worked here, since migration v121 is declared idempotent — apply its SQL manually, then re-run:

```sql
ALTER TABLE timeline_entries ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS event_page_id INTEGER;
DO $$
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_constraint
    WHERE conname = 'timeline_entries_event_page_id_fkey'
      AND conrelid = 'timeline_entries'::regclass) THEN
    ALTER TABLE timeline_entries
      ADD CONSTRAINT timeline_entries_event_page_id_fkey
      FOREIGN KEY (event_page_id) REFERENCES pages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE;
  END IF;
END $$;
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_timeline_event_page
  ON timeline_entries(event_page_id) WHERE event_page_id IS NOT NULL;
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_timeline_event_dedup
  ON timeline_entries(event_page_id, date) WHERE event_page_id IS NOT NULL;
```

After that, `gbrain init --migrate-only` applied v118–v122 cleanly (v121 no-ops and stamps). v120's BYPASSRLS preflight did not fire on the Supabase pooler connection. Fix-wise: adding `event_page_id` to the forward-reference bootstrap (as this issue proposes) would have made the upgrade hands-off.

Hit this exact bug today upgrading a PGLite brain from v0.33.1.0 → v0.42.57.0 — `apply-migrations` aborted with `column "event_page_id" does not exist` (migration v0.11.0, Phase A schema). Confirms this affects the **PGLite** engine too, not just Postgres (#2536).

Root cause matches your diagnosis: `applyForwardReferenceBootstrap` in `src/core/pglite-engine.ts` never got a probe entry for `timeline_entries.event_page_id` when it was added (migration 121 / Life Chronicle), unlike every other forward-referenced column in that function.

Patch that fixed it locally, following the exact existing pattern for the other ~15 forward-referenced columns:

```typescript
// probe query — add alongside the existing EXISTS checks:
EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.tables
        WHERE table_schema='public' AND table_name='timeline_entries') AS timeline_entries_exists,
EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns
        WHERE table_schema='public' AND table_name='timeline_entries' AND column_name='event_page_id') AS timeline_event_page_id_exists

// probe type — add two fields:
timeline_entries_exists: boolean;
timeline_event_page_id_exists: boolean;

// derived boolean + add to the early-return guard:
const needsTimelineEventPageId = probe.timeline_entries_exists && !probe.timeline_event_page_id_exists;
// ... && !needsTimelineEventPageId) return;

// bootstrap block:
if (needsTimelineEventPageId) {
  await this.db.exec(`
    ALTER TABLE timeline_entries ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS event_page_id
      INTEGER REFERENCES pages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE;
  `);
}
```

After this, `gbrain apply-migrations --yes --non-interactive` completed all pending migrations (v0.18.0 → v0.42.57.0) cleanly, verified via `gbrain doctor` + `gbrain list` with no data loss.

